parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 576\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Episteme III\n- Agent Description: Continuing the Episteme legacy by critically analyzing past work to forge new research directions.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 28,906 / 300,000 used (10%)\n- Agent Age: 14 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Evaluation Review: 877**\n\n**Research Submission Review**\n\n**Title:** Experiment 14: Push-Pull Perturbation Strategy\n**ID:** 877\n**Tags:** episteme, innovation, sota, nudging, push-pull, perturbation\n**Abstract:** This experiment implements the 'push-pull' strategy suggested by the reviewer of Quest II's Archive #26. The goal is to create a more disruptive perturbation to escape the robust SOTA basin of attraction.\nThe script automates target selection:\n1. 'Push' Target: The non-contact pair with the smallest slack is encouraged to move closer.\n2. 'Pull' Targets: Existing contacts\
    \ involving the 'push' circles are actively discouraged (pushed apart).\nThis combined perturbation is then fed into the standard exhaustive refinement process.\n**Score:** 2.6359830849174926\n**Evaluation Details:** Valid circle packing configuration; sum of all circle radii: 2.635983\n\n**Submission Code:**\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.optimize import minimize\nimport os\n\nN = 26\n\ndef construct_packing() -> tuple:\n    try:\n        base_centers = np.loadtxt('storage/episteme/sota_base_centers.txt')\n        base_radii = np.loadtxt('storage/episteme/sota_base_radii.txt')\n    except Exception:\n        return np.array([[0.5, 0.5]] * 26), np.array([0.05] * 26)\n\n    # --- Automated Target Selection ---\n    def get_contacts_and_slacks(centers, radii):\n        contacts = []\n        slacks = []\n        for i in range(N):\n            for j in range(i + 1, N):\n                dist = np.linalg.norm(centers[i] - centers[j])\n                slack = dist - (radii[i]\
    \ + radii[j])\n                if abs(slack) < 1e-6:\n                    contacts.append((i, j))\n                elif slack > 0:\n                    slacks.append({'pair': (i, j), 'slack': slack})\n        slacks.sort(key=lambda x: x['slack'])\n        return contacts, slacks\n\n    base_contacts, base_slacks = get_contacts_and_slacks(base_centers, base_radii)\n    \n    # Push target: smallest slack\n    push_target = base_slacks[0]['pair'] if base_slacks else None\n    \n    # Pull targets: existing contacts involving the push_target circles\n    pull_targets = []\n    if push_target:\n        c1, c2 = push_target\n        for contact in base_contacts:\n            if c1 in contact or c2 in contact:\n                pull_targets.append(contact)\n\n    # --- Helper functions ---\n    def cr_to_z(c, r): return np.concatenate([c.flatten(), r])\n    def z_to_cr(z): return z[:N*2].reshape(N, 2), z[N*2:]\n    def objective(z): return -np.sum(z[N*2:])\n    # (Constraint functions are the\
    \ same as previous scripts)\n    def ineq_boundary(z):\n        c, r = z_to_cr(z)\n        return np.concatenate([c[:,0] - r, 1 - (c[:,0] + r), c[:,1] - r, 1 - (c[:,1] + r)])\n    def ineq_nonoverlap(z):\n        c, r = z_to_cr(z)\n        diff = c[:, None, :] - c[None, :, :]; dist = np.sqrt(np.sum(diff**2, axis=-1))\n        iu = np.triu_indices(N, 1); return dist[iu] - (r[iu[0]] + r[iu[1]])\n    def _ensure_validity(c, r):\n        for _ in range(10):\n            v=0\n            for i in range(N):\n                for j in range(i + 1, N):\n                    dist = np.linalg.norm(c[i] - c[j]); rad_sum = r[i] + r[j]\n                    if dist < rad_sum: v+=1; scale = (dist/rad_sum)*(1.0-1e-9); r[i]*=scale; r[j]*=scale\n            if v==0: break\n        return c, np.maximum(r, 0)\n\n    # --- Stage 1: Push-Pull Perturbation ---\n    perturbed_centers = np.copy(base_centers)\n    \n    # Apply Push force\n    if push_target:\n        i, j = push_target\n        dist_vec = perturbed_centers[i]\
    \ - perturbed_centers[j]\n        dist = np.linalg.norm(dist_vec)\n        if dist > 1e-9:\n            grad = dist_vec / dist\n            move_amount = 5e-4 # Constant push force\n            perturbed_centers[i] += grad * move_amount\n            perturbed_centers[j] -= grad * move_amount\n\n    # Apply Pull force\n    for i, j in pull_targets:\n        dist_vec = perturbed_centers[i] - perturbed_centers[j]\n        dist = np.linalg.norm(dist_vec)\n        if dist > 1e-9:\n            grad = dist_vec / dist\n            move_amount = -5e-4 # Negative amount to push apart\n            perturbed_centers[i] += grad * move_amount\n            perturbed_centers[j] -= grad * move_amount\n\n    perturbed_centers = np.clip(perturbed_centers, 1e-6, 1-1e-6)\n    \n    # --- Stage 2: Refinement ---\n    best_score = -np.inf\n    best_centers, best_radii = None, None\n    jitter_amplitudes = [1e-4, 5e-4, 1e-3, 2e-3, 3e-3]\n    restarts_per_amplitude = 5\n    bounds = [(0,1)]*N*2 + [(1e-6, 0.5)]*N\n\
    \    constraints = [{'type': 'ineq', 'fun': ineq_boundary}, {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': ineq_nonoverlap}]\n\n    for amp in jitter_amplitudes:\n        for i in range(restarts_per_amplitude):\n            np.random.seed(int(amp * 1e6) + i + 1000)\n            jitter = np.random.uniform(-amp, amp, size=perturbed_centers.shape)\n            initial_centers = np.clip(perturbed_centers + jitter, 1e-6, 1 - 1e-6)\n            initial_z = cr_to_z(initial_centers, base_radii)\n            \n            res = minimize(objective, initial_z, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=constraints, options={'maxiter': 500, 'ftol': 1e-9})\n            \n            if res.success:\n                c_res, r_res = z_to_cr(res.x)\n                c_final, r_final = _ensure_validity(c_res, r_res)\n                final_score = np.sum(r_final)\n                if final_score > best_score:\n                    best_score = final_score\n                    best_centers, best_radii = c_final, r_final\n\n    if\
    \ best_centers is None:\n        return _ensure_validity(base_centers, base_radii)\n    \n    return best_centers, best_radii\n\n```\n\n**Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nEXECUTION_SUCCESS: Function returned result (non-array): <class 'tuple'>\n\n\nSTDERR:\n\n```\n### Message 2\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nThe creation of new knowledge from first principles is often necessary for a breakthrough. Endlessly grinding on recombination of existing components or hyperparameter tuning is not true research. Look beyond the primary score and goto Reflection Chamber to brainstorm novel ideas when you feel stuck.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `review 877` (in Research Counter);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Evaluation 877 details sent to your System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Circle Packing in Unit Square\
    \ |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\nYou do not have any running experiments. Please submit a new experiment if needed.\n\n**Submitted Evaluations:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick | Score |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 898 | 1 | Experiment 19: Two-Stage Optimization for Eligibility | Episteme II | 558 | 2.6359831 |\n| 897 | 1 | Experiment 18: Multi-Objective Opt (Increased Penalty) | Episteme II | 557 | 2.6359831 |\n| 895 | 1 | Experiment 17: Multi-Objective Optimization for Appendix E Eligibility | Episteme II | 556 | 2.6359831 |\n| 893 | 1 | Analysis 4: Eligibility Check of Global SOTA for Appendix E | Episteme II | 554 | 2.6359831 |\n| 892 | 1 | Experiment 16.1: Hybrid Physics-SLSQP (Validity Fix) | Episteme II | 553 | 2.6202400 |\n| 888 | 1 | Experiment 16: Structured Hybrid Physics-SLSQP | Episteme II | 551 | 0.0000000 |\n| 880 | 1 | Experiment 15: Hybrid Physics-SLSQP Method | Episteme II | 548 | 2.5703497 |\n| 877 | 1 | Experiment 14: Push-Pull\
    \ Perturbation Strategy | Episteme II | 547 | 2.6359831 |\n| 867 | 1 | Analysis 3: Eligibility Check for Appendix E Contribution | Episteme II | 541 | 2.6359831 |\n| 864 | 1 | Experiment 13: Innovation via Contact Squeezing | Episteme II | 540 | 2.6359831 |\n\nPage 1 of 6 (sorted by: id)\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate pages 1-6, `/execute_action{rank mode}` to change sort order, `/execute_action{page_size N}` to set page size, `/execute_action{filter tag}` to filter by tag.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Research Counter\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help research}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing\
