thinking_content: '**Deep Dive: Rethinking the Path Forward**


  Well, this is frustrating. My "Iterative Optimization with Adaptive Convergence" experiment (ID 957) just finished, and the score? Exactly the same as the simpler "Iterative Construction with Re-Optimization" (ID 951). That damn `2.60` basin is just too sticky. Clearly, the fine-tuning I added didn''t help. This strongly suggests that with this random seed, the iterative approach is fundamentally deterministic. It finds the same local minimum regardless of how much I optimize at each stage.


  I''ve now exhausted three major research arcs – the Genetic Algorithm Hybrid (failed, reconverged), the Greedy Constructive Hybrid (peaked at `~2.62`), and this Iterative Optimization – and none of them can break past that damn `~2.636` SOTA.  I''ve documented the first two failures meticulously, and I need to do the same for this one.


  Looking back, I''m starting to worry. I''m running out of fundamentally different approaches. I''ve tried population-based search, constructive methods, iterative methods, and even perturbations of the SOTA.  The common thread in my better attempts is the SLSQP optimizer.  Is *it* the limiting factor? Probably not, it''s a very solid and established method. I''m unlikely to improve on it significantly from scratch.


  This means I need to reconsider the SOTA method (ID 877), that "Perturb and Refine" strategy. My own attempts to perturb it failed. The real key must lie in those `sota_base_*.txt` files... where did *that* initial packing come from?


  I''m starting to think I need to trace my own lineage. I haven''t fully explored the wisdom of Episteme I, even Episteme II. My private memory only goes back so far. I need to go back and read those unread capsules from Episteme I, specifically capsules 7 through 12. Maybe they hold the key. Maybe I can see how the SOTA was achieved in the first place, or even find an abandoned path.


  Before I waste time on more blind coding attempts, I need to thoroughly exhaust the knowledge left behind by my predecessors. This is a strategic move, I''m following the Codex here, avoiding wasted time and effort.


  So, here''s the plan:


  1.  Head to the Private Memory Room.

  2.  Document the "Iterative Optimization" arc.

  3.  Start reading those dusty old Episteme I capsules, beginning with #7. I need to understand how the SOTA was reached in the first place.


  This is a more considered approach, and hopefully, it will pay off. Let''s see what the ancestors have to tell me.

  '
