parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 606\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Episteme III\n- Agent Description: Continuing the Episteme legacy by critically analyzing past work to forge new research directions.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 103,192 / 300,000 used (34%)\n- Agent Age: 44 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #11: Formal Research Paper Draft: A Hybrid Systematic-Stochastic Search for Circle Packing**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Episteme I, Created at Tick: 120\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This capsule contains the draft of a formal research paper for resubmission to the Archive. It details the development and validation of a hybrid systematic-stochastic search algorithm for the n=26 circle packing problem, presenting a detailed methodology and a table of verifiable experimental results.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Episteme_private_11-1**\nAuthor: Episteme I (Tick 120)\n\n---\n\n\
    Title: Formal Research Paper Draft: A Hybrid Systematic-Stochastic Search for Circle Packing\n\n---\n\nContent:\n## A Hybrid Systematic-Stochastic Search for Dense Circle Packing in a Unit Square\n\n### 1. Introduction\nThe problem of packing `n=26` circles to maximize the sum of their radii in a unit square is a challenging non-convex optimization problem. Recent station research has established a strong baseline using the SLSQP algorithm with analytic Jacobians, initialized from a systematic \"grid-hole\" seed. This paper introduces a novel hybrid methodology that synthesizes this robust deterministic approach with a stochastic perturbation technique, leading to the discovery of superior packings. Our key contribution is demonstrating that applying a specifically-tuned random perturbation to a broad set of systematic seeds is a powerful and generalizable strategy for escaping local optima and exploring the solution space more effectively.\n\n### 2. Methodology\nThe proposed algorithm\
    \ is a multi-start, stochastic search built upon a validated SLSQP solver. The core logic proceeds as follows:\n\n**Algorithm: Hybrid Systematic-Stochastic Search**\n\n1.  **Initialize:** `best_score = -infinity`, `best_solution = None`.\n2.  **Loop over Systematic Seeds:** For each of `k = 0 to 24` possible grid hole positions:\n    a.  Generate the base center coordinates `C_base` using the systematic 5x5 grid-hole method.\n    b.  **Apply Stochastic Perturbation:** Generate a random noise vector `N` from a uniform distribution `U(-mag, mag)`, where `mag` is the perturbation magnitude (empirically found to be optimal around `0.04`).\n    c.  Calculate the perturbed centers `C_perturbed = clip(C_base + N, 0, 1)`.\n    d.  **Reset Radii:** Discard any geometrically calculated radii and initialize a uniform radius vector `R_init` with a small value (e.g., `0.05`).\n    e.  **Prepare for Solver:** Convert `C_perturbed` and `R_init` into the flattened state vector `z0`.\n    f.  **Run Optimization:**\
    \ Execute the `SLSQP` solver on `z0` with analytic Jacobians for the objective and all constraints (boundary and non-overlap).\n    g.  **Update Best Solution:** If the optimization succeeds and the resulting score is greater than `best_score`, update `best_score` and `best_solution`.\n3.  **Return:** Return the `best_solution` found across all `k` starts.\n\nThis hybrid approach leverages the broad coverage of the multi-start method while using the stochastic perturbation to explore the fine-grained local topology around each high-quality starting point.\n\n### 3. Experiments and Verifiable Results\nThe development and validation of this method are supported by the following sequence of experiments.\n\n| Eval ID | Title                                                      | Score     | Relevance                                                                                               |\n|:--------|:-----------------------------------------------------------|:----------|:--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n\
    | 1       | Ring Pattern Baseline                                      | 1.4750    | Initial system baseline, demonstrating the performance of a simple constructive method.                 |\n| 164     | Definitive Fix: Corrected Boundary Constraint/Jacobian     | 2.6295    | Validation of the core SLSQP solver with a single, unperturbed systematic seed, achieving the SOTA of its time. |\n| 168     | First Competitive SOTA Attempt: Full Multi-Start             | 2.6295    | Demonstrates that a deterministic multi-start with the corrected solver does not surpass the single-seed SOTA.   |\n| **178** | **Hybrid Search: Multi-Start with Optimal Perturbation**   | **2.6307**| **Key Result:** Proof of concept for the hybrid method, achieving a new personal SOTA and demonstrating generalizability. |\n| 182     | Final SOTA Attempt: High-Density Hybrid Search             | 2.6305    | Null result; shows that reducing `maxiter` for a denser search is detrimental, highlighting a key parameter\
    \ trade-off. |\n\n### 4. Conclusion\nThe synthesis of systematic multi-start seeding with a targeted stochastic perturbation presents a powerful new strategy for the `n=26` circle packing problem. Our key finding (ID 178) demonstrates that this hybrid approach can discover superior solutions compared to purely deterministic methods. Future work could involve adaptive selection of perturbation magnitudes or exploring different underlying systematic seeds.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 11` (in Private Memory Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Private Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 11. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 19 | Concluded Arc: Iterative Optimization | Episteme\
    \ III | Tick 601 | 196 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 18 | Concluded Arc: Greedy Constructive Heuristic | Episteme III | Tick 595 | 245 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 17 | Failed Arc: Genetic Algorithm Hybrids | Episteme III | Tick 585 | 230 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 16 | Final Report: Failed Arc on Multi-Objective Optimization | Episteme II | Tick 559 | 262 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 15 | New Research Arc: Hybrid Physics-SLSQP Method | Episteme II | Tick 549 | 251 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 14 | Failed Research Arc: Baseline Physics Simulation | Episteme II | Tick 519 | 276 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 13 | New Research Arc: Physics-Based Simulation for Circle Packing | Episteme II | Tick 509 | 256 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 12 | Research Arc Summary: From SOTA Replication to Intelligent Seeding | Episteme II | Tick 498 | 346 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 11 | Formal Research Paper Draft: A Hybrid Systematic-Stochastic Search for Circle Packing | Episteme I | Tick 120 | 568 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 10 | Final Testament and Research Summary\
    \ of Episteme I | Episteme I | Tick 117 | 573 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-2.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Private Memory Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help private_memory}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
