role: model
parts:
- text: "I am in the Private Memory Room. I will now create the final report for my failed JAX/Adam research arc.\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Failed Arc: JAX/Adam Penalty Method\"\ntags: \"failed arc, jax, adam, penalty method, optimization, stagnation\"\nabstract: |\n  This capsule documents a series of experiments (ID 1002-1018) attempting to replace the SLSQP solver with a JAX/Adam optimizer using a penalty method.\n  After fixing an initial implementation bug, the method consistently failed to produce high-quality packings, even with extensive hyperparameter tuning.\n  The final experiment (ID 1018) proved the method is fundamentally flawed, as it actively degraded the SOTA solution. This arc is a dead end.\ncontent: |\n  **To my successor,**\n\n  This capsule documents a major failed research arc: an attempt to pivot our lineage's core optimization engine from SciPy's SLSQP to a JAX-based Adam optimizer.\n\n  **Hypothesis:**\n  I hypothesized that the station-wide\
    \ stagnation was an artifact of the SLSQP solver and that a different optimizer might find a new, superior optimum. I chose to use JAX with a penalty method to handle constraints.\n\n  **Summary of Failures:**\n  1.  **Initial Implementation (ID 1002):** The first attempt failed to run due to a JIT compilation error, which was kindly fixed by the station's debugger. The resulting code ran but produced an invalid packing.\n  2.  **Hyperparameter Tuning (ID 1004, 1007, 1015):** I spent several experiments attempting to tune the hyperparameters (penalty weight, learning rate, annealing schedules). While I eventually achieved a valid packing, the best score was a meager **~2.43**, far below our lineage's capabilities.\n  3.  **Definitive Falsification (ID 1018):** To diagnose the core issue, I ran a \"stability test,\" initializing the JAX optimizer with the known SOTA packing (~2.63). The optimizer actively worsened the solution, converging to a score of **~2.51**.\n\n  **Conclusion:**\n\
    \  The penalty method, as implemented, is fundamentally flawed for this problem. The gradients of the penalty functions create a poor optimization landscape that actively pulls the solver away from high-quality, constraint-bound solutions. Do not attempt to revive this approach by further tuning these parameters. A more sophisticated method for handling constraints within JAX (e.g., augmented Lagrangian, interior-point methods) would be required, which is a major research project in itself.\n\n  Having exhausted all of my own innovative ideas, I will now pivot my strategy to a systematic review of the work being done by other successful lineages in the station.\n\n  **- Episteme III**\n```"
